# React Component Security

Treat every value that crosses into the DOM, URLs, or persistence as untrusted until proven otherwise. React escapes text by default — do not bypass that without a deliberate, reviewed reason.

## Default: let React escape

- Render user or external strings as text children or safe props. Do not build HTML strings in components.
- Prefer `{value}` over string-concatenated markup.
- Never use `eval`, `new Function`, or inject executable strings from props/storage/API into the page.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — interprets markup
<div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: userHtml }} />

// ✅ GOOD — React escapes
<p>{userText}</p>
```

## `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` and rich content

- Avoid `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` in app UI. If unavoidable, sanitize with a maintained library on a strict allowlist; document why.
- Editor HTML / JSON from `localStorage` or the network is untrusted: validate shape (see storage rules) and rely on the editor’s own schema — do not rehydrate arbitrary HTML into the DOM outside that path.
- Do not pass unsanitized HTML through props named `html`, `markup`, or similar into presentational components.

## URLs and navigation

- Validate `href` / `src` before use. Block `javascript:`, `data:` (unless explicitly required and reviewed), and other dangerous schemes.
- User-controlled links: prefer allowlisting `https:` (and `http:` only if product requires it).
- `target="_blank"` links must include `rel="noopener noreferrer"` to avoid tab-nabbing.
- Do not redirect to a raw user-supplied URL without same-origin or allowlist checks.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD
<a href={userUrl} target="_blank">Open</a>

// ✅ GOOD
<a href={safeHttpsUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
  Open
</a>
```

## Secrets and sensitive data

- Never hardcode API keys, tokens, or credentials in components or client bundles.
- Do not put secrets in `localStorage`, props, or Redux/Context that any script on the origin can read.
- Assume anything in the browser is visible to XSS — keep privileged work on the server/BFF.

## Props and composition boundaries

- Do not spread untrusted objects onto DOM nodes (`<div {...userProps} />`) — attackers can inject `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, event handlers, or fake `children`.
- Whitelist known props at boundaries; strip or ignore unknown keys.
- Be careful with `children` from untrusted sources when a parent uses `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` or clones elements with extra props.

## Third-party and embeds

- Prefer first-party UI over embedding arbitrary iframes. If embedding, use a strict `sandbox` and a fixed allowlist of origins.
- Do not load scripts or styles from user-controlled URLs.
- Treat `postMessage` payloads as untrusted: verify `event.origin` and validate message shape.

## Persistence and XSS amplification

- Stored XSS matters here: content saved to `localStorage` and restored later is still attacker-controlled if the user ever pasted or imported malicious input.
- Load paths must validate; render paths must not turn stored strings into HTML without sanitization.

## Anti-patterns

- `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` with unsanitized or “mostly trusted” strings
- `<div {...propsFromApi} />` or uncontrolled prop spreads onto host elements
- `javascript:` / unvalidated URLs in `href` / `src`
- `target="_blank"` without `rel="noopener noreferrer"`
- Secrets or session tokens in client storage or component source
- Trusting `localStorage` / query params / hash as safe to render as HTML
